// src/v4/extdeps/languages/python.dag
//
// Scope: Python target language spec — primitives, syntax, codegen rules.
// Anchor: https://docs.python.org/3.14/reference/ (The Python Language
//         Reference, version 3.14 — pinned at the current stable
//         release as of work date; the L-2 versioned fidelity
//         authority). The pinned `/3.14/` form is REQUIRED rather than
//         the floating `/3/` (per operator BLOCKING review on PR #3180:
//         extdeps.md requires a declared spec version so upstream
//         Python changes cannot silently mutate this substrate). The
//         carriers below — PythonNumericTower, PythonStringKind,
//         PythonSingletonKind, PythonScalar — are pinned to this
//         release; when a future Python release modifies the standard
//         type hierarchy (a new singleton type joins; the numeric-tower
//         ABC partition is revised; a new sequence-kind enters the
//         textual-types section), the change is a visible C1 edit to
//         the relevant declaration below + a coordinated version-pin
//         bump on THIS line, never a silent extension via the floating
//         URL. The per-carrier `// Spec:` URLs below mirror the pin.
// Unifies: (per THESIS:188) language spec = transport spec = interpreter
//         runtime. ONE substrate carrier for all three roles. Different
//         lenses read different facts from the same data (emission
//         reads syntax; transport reads serialization; interpreter
//         reads execution semantics). A worker needing transport or
//         runtime extends this file, not a new "transport spec" file.
//
// STRUCTURAL FINDING (this slice's deliverable, manager directive
//   msg_4f7e1280 fan-out — same shape as PR #3174 rust slice
//   established 2026-05-16).
//   Python's TYPE-SYSTEM SURFACE — scalar primitive vocabulary, numeric
//   subtype tower, singleton types — fits the substrate's existing
//   classifier shape (closed Disj + Conj records) without modification.
//   No 7th connective, no 6th behavior is surfaced by this slice. The
//   slice declares the Python type VOCABULARY as Node-shaped
//   declaration-class data; it deliberately STOPS at the carrier-
//   shaped seams where D1 (parse/emit operations — ratified
//   `Outcome<T> = Produced { value: T } | Rejected { diagnostic:
//   Diagnostic }`, NOT YET LANDED in std/diagnostic.dag) and D2 (per-
//   language primitive inhabitance instance-values + fn-value-
//   expression form — operator-pending SEMANTIC decision, msg_640d429c;
//   §0 ratifies v2-syntax legality only, NOT the inhabitance shape
//   per msg_8b477982) plug in.
//
//   The seams are NAMED in "Owned ELSEWHERE" below with their owners
//   and dissolution triggers. Authoring those seams here would have
//   improvised past D1/D2 — exactly the keen-wren-419 (T-4.6) /
//   loyal-koi-702 (T-4 rust) STOP-and-escalate precedent established
//   2026-05-16. Deferral-with-named-trigger, never improvisation.
//
//   PYTHON-SPECIFIC STRUCTURAL NOTE (the substrate fit's Python
//   particulars vs the rust analog):
//   (a) NO WIDTH AXIS. Python `int` is arbitrary-precision (Reference
//       §3.2 numeric-types-int — "Integers have unlimited precision");
//       `float` is "machine-level double precision" with
//       IMPLEMENTATION CAVEATS the spec explicitly names (Reference
//       §3.2 numeric-types-float — "You are at the mercy of the
//       underlying machine architecture (and C or Java implementation)
//       for the accepted range and handling of overflow") — the spec
//       does NOT mandate IEEE-754 binary64, only the canonical
//       CPython realization does; modeling `float` as a fixed
//       IEEE-754 width would over-promise spec fidelity (operator
//       BLOCKING on PR #3180 made this precise); `complex` is a pair
//       of `float` carrying the same machine-level caveat. The kind
//       × width admissibility axis that drove rust.dag's RustScalar
//       Disj-with-payloads design (Bits8/16/32/64/128/Pointer × Signed/
//       Unsigned) DOES NOT EXIST in Python. The Python typed-scalar
//       axis collapses to the kind alone, structurally simpler than
//       rust by spec design.
//   (b) NUMERIC SUBTYPE TOWER. Python's numerics have a STRUCTURALLY
//       PARTIAL-ORDERED hierarchy (PEP 285 — `bool` is a subtype of
//       `int`; PEP 3141 — Integral ⊆ Rational ⊆ Real ⊆ Complex). The
//       admissibility axis Python ADDS (vs rust which has no such
//       subtype relationship between scalar kinds) is the numeric-
//       tower partition, declared below as a closed 4-way classifier.
//   (c) THREE SINGLETON TYPES. Reference §3.2 names exactly three
//       singleton scalars — `None` (NoneType), `NotImplemented`
//       (NotImplementedType), `Ellipsis` (EllipsisType). Each has
//       exactly one inhabitant per the spec; the empty-cardinality
//       fact rides std/cardinality.dag (T-3 wave A3).
//   (d) MUTABILITY DEGENERATE AT SCALAR LAYER. Among Python's scalar
//       kinds, only `bytearray` is mutable; the other nine are
//       structurally immutable per the Reference. A separate
//       PythonMutability axis would be degenerate at the scalar
//       layer — only meaningful at the container layer (list / set
//       / dict, scope-negative for this slice).
//
// Owns (this slice — REVISED from scaffold; scaffold's "Python
//   primitives + data model" + "emission rules" + "cost realization
//   for Python" lines are PRESERVED AS GOALS in the Owns→ELSEWHERE
//   table below; the slice-vs-bundled split is explicit, not silent —
//   same revision discipline as rust.dag #3174 and ptx.dag #3170):
//
//   - Python numeric subtype tower: PythonNumericTower (Bool /
//     Integral / Real / Complex — the closed 4-level PEP 285 + PEP 3141
//     partition, with the partial-order Bool ⊑ Integral ⊑ Real ⊑
//     Complex structurally derived from the closed enum)
//   - Python string/byte-sequence kind: PythonStringKind (Str / Bytes /
//     Bytearray — the closed 3-way Reference §3.2 textual / byte-
//     sequence partition; mutability is fixed per variant —
//     Str/Bytes immutable, Bytearray mutable — STRUCTURALLY
//     UNREPRESENTABLE to mix)
//   - Python singleton kind: PythonSingletonKind (NoneT /
//     NotImplementedT / EllipsisT — the closed 3-way Reference §3.2
//     singleton-types partition; each has exactly one inhabitant)
//   - Python typed-scalar sum: PythonScalar (the 3-variant Disj with
//     named-field nested-kind payloads — Numeric / String /
//     Singleton; serves BOTH the top-level 3-category kind
//     classifier role AND the typed-value role in one carrier
//     because Python's spec has no orthogonal width axis for the
//     typed-value layer to add — see PythonScalar's modeling note
//     for the Python-vs-rust structural-difference rationale.
//     Illegal cross-category combinations like "a Singleton with a
//     numeric tower level" are STRUCTURALLY UNREPRESENTABLE, because
//     each variant carries exactly the sub-classifier its category
//     admits)
//   - Python per-target cost-realization carrier shape: PythonCost
//     (per-spec-operation + per-allocation components per Reference
//     §6/§8 — NOT per CPython bytecode op, which is implementation
//     detail per the `dis` module disclaimer — the per-target
//     projection U1 reads; the carrier SHAPE only, no instance-values
//     pre-T-3 / pre-D2)
//
// Owned ELSEWHERE (deliberately NOT in this slice — named owners +
//   dissolution triggers per directive item 3 "DEFER explicitly, with
//   named owner + dissolution trigger"; precedent: rust.dag #3174,
//   ptx.dag #3170):
//
//   - Python scalar inhabitance instance-values: `data
//     python_int_integral: <Integral-shape>(<int-carrier>) = …` and
//     the parallel rows for float / bool / complex (numeric tower
//     groundings), str (FreeMonoid<UnicodeScalarValue>), bytes /
//     bytearray (FreeMonoid<Byte>), and the singleton cardinality
//     witnesses. RIDES the operator D2 form-decision (msg_640d429c —
//     STOP-2/STOP-3 escalated by loyal-koi-702; brief §B's per-
//     language inhabitance form conflicts with INVARIANTS P1:42's
//     `Compose<Int, MachineWidth<Word*>>` worked example AND the
//     fn-value-expression form for spec-primitive operations
//     (3a/3b/3c — name-reference Atom vs relaxed instance vs new
//     substrate carrier) is unspecified). §0 ratifies v2-SYNTAX
//     LEGALITY ONLY; per-target inhabitance shape is a SEPARATE
//     operator-pending SEMANTIC decision (manager reinforcement
//     msg_8b477982). AND rides std/integer.dag / std/float.dag /
//     std/logic.dag / std/text.dag (T-3 wave A3) carrier landing for
//     P2 single-authority: pre-D2 / pre-T-3 inhabitance would
//     foreclose the eventual canonical authority.
//     STOP-TRIGGER (named, binding): authoring even a "placeholder"
//     `data python_int_integral: <…> = …` row here pre-D2 is the
//     FORBIDDEN deferral (the v3 cross-layer-drift failure mode this
//     file's restraint exists to prevent). Stub-to-keep-moving is
//     forbidden; defer-with-named-trigger is the legitimate form.
//     SEAM (where it plugs in): the closed PythonScalar Disj
//     declared below is the SURFACE through which each Python scalar's
//     inhabitance parameterizes — when D2 ratifies the instance-value
//     form AND T-3 wave A3 lands, the inhabitance rows land in this
//     file with the typed scalar variants below as their carriers.
//
//   - Bidirectional Python LanguageModel substrate (grammar
//     productions as declarative B2-OMNI data — Python's PEG grammar
//     per Reference §10 full-grammar; C5-fidelity disposition tags
//     per surface feature, esp. SIGNIFICANT-WHITESPACE-IS-BLOCK
//     `Modeled` since Python indentation IS block structure per
//     TASKS.md T-4 authoring contract; ingest∘emit roundtrip
//     carrier): the BUNDLED work of T-4 across all 5 Shape A source
//     languages, where "the SHAPE is the work" (TASKS.md T-4
//     authoring contract). RIDES the operator D1 value-or-Diagnostic
//     carrier — `Outcome<T> = Produced { value: T } | Rejected
//     { diagnostic: Diagnostic }`, RATIFIED 2026-05-16 (manager
//     reinforcement msg_8b477982, ratified shape), NOT YET LANDED in
//     std/diagnostic.dag. Parse / emit operations cannot type their
//     failure mode without it. When the carrier lands AND the
//     bundled LanguageModel shape lands, this file's classifiers
//     slot into the Python `LanguageModel` instance without
//     revision; their I/O contract is the immutable header above.
//     STOP-TRIGGER (named, binding): the temptation to STUB an
//     `emit_python : Node -> Outcome<PythonSource>` op here while
//     D1 is unlanded is the FORBIDDEN deferral (the v3 failure
//     mode the value-or-Diagnostic-STOP precedent foreclosed) —
//     STOP and surface, never stub. Stub-to-keep-moving is the
//     exact shape that broke v3; the named "Owned ELSEWHERE" note
//     is the legitimate deferral form (rust #3174 / ptx #3170
//     precedent).
//     SEAM: the Python grammar production data attaches as Node
//     trees in the LanguageModel file (B2-OMNI: grammar is data,
//     not code); the carriers declared here are the type-system
//     structure the grammar references when productions resolve
//     type expressions.
//
//   - Pipeline emission/ingest operations (`.dag` Node → Python
//     source; Python source → Node ingest with C5-fidelity
//     classification per surface feature, esp. indentation-as-block-
//     structure per the T-4 authoring contract). RIDES the ratified
//     `Outcome<T>` D1 carrier directly (when it lands in
//     std/diagnostic.dag). Owners: compiler/05_emit.dag (T-10),
//     compiler/02_parse.dag (T-7) — emission and ingest are
//     pipeline-stage operations, NOT facts of this language file.
//     The LanguageModel data DECLARES the bidirectional rules; the
//     pipeline stages EXECUTE them.
//     STOP-TRIGGER (named, binding): pre-D1 authoring of
//     `emit_python : Node -> Outcome<PythonSource>` here would
//     parallel-represent the Outcome carrier across every language
//     file — exactly the v3 cross-layer-drift failure mode the
//     keen-wren-419 STOP (T-4.6 formats, msg_0da56c18) foreclosed.
//     Defer-with-named-trigger, never stub.
//
//   - Python async / await semantics (`async def`, `.await`,
//     coroutines, async generators, async iterators): the COROUTINE
//     and ASYNC-ITERATION carriers compose over the type-system
//     declarations here (an `async def` returns a coroutine-typed
//     value), but the coordination-effect typing is owned by
//     extdeps/coordination.dag (T-4.8, IN-B effect-typed Bind
//     carriers — the same substrate that owns Rust's async / Verilog
//     concurrency / ptx SIMT-Bind effect typing); not authored here.
//
//   - Python exception flow (`raise` / `try / except / finally`):
//     RIDES the ratified `Outcome<T>` D1 carrier — Python's
//     exception is a structural error path on a typed return, and
//     the carrier is exactly the Outcome shape D1 ratifies. The
//     pipeline-stage operations (T-7 parse / T-10 emit) plug the
//     exception flow into Outcome when D1 lands; not authored here.
//
//   - Python duck-typing / structural-typing surface (protocols per
//     PEP 544, `typing.Protocol`). Python's nominal class hierarchy
//     is what `class X:` declares; structural typing through
//     protocols is RUNTIME structural-compatibility, modeled at the
//     LanguageModel grammar layer (T-4 bundled work) when type
//     annotations parse. The numeric-tower partition declared here
//     IS a structural sub-typing fact at the BUILT-IN-spec layer
//     (PEP 3141 ABC hierarchy is part of the language reference, not
//     a library protocol); user-defined protocols ride T-4 grammar.
//
//   - Python per-spec-operation cost INSTANCE-VALUES (concrete
//     `PythonCost { operation_cost: 1, allocation_cost: 0 }` rows per
//     spec-defined operation — the Reference §6 expressions / §8
//     statements partition; NOT per CPython-bytecode op, which is
//     CPython implementation detail per the `dis` module docs and
//     varies across versions / alternative VMs (PyPy / Jython /
//     GraalPython / MicroPython)): the carrier SHAPE is owned here
//     (PythonCost below); concrete cost rows ride T-12 lens/cost.dag
//     + U2 (the per-target projection of U1's `Homomorphism<…, …,
//     PythonScalar>.map`). Pre-T-12 instance-values would parallel-
//     represent the cost the map structurally encodes — the U1
//     no-engine smell (algebra.dag U1).
//
// Consumes:
//   - nothing — this file is closed-enum + Conj-record declarations
//     only. `Int` is kernel-ambient (the PythonCost components' raw
//     Int carrier per the 🟡 scaffold bridge to std/cardinality.dag);
//     `Bool` is kernel-ambient (referenced in commentary on sum-shape
//     arguments). No module is imported — this file is closed-enum +
//     Conj-record declarations only; unlike rust.dag #3174 which
//     imports `Symbol` from std/node.dag for its lifetime field on
//     RustReference, Python has no reference-kind axis and no
//     lifetime parameter (every Python name is a reference; mutability
//     is the OBJECT's property, not the reference's), so the
//     `Symbol`-on-reference shape doesn't apply here.
//   (The scaffold header's prior `std/node.dag, std/algebra.dag
//   (same as rust.dag)` Consumes line was anticipatory — both were
//   named for the inhabitance instance-values this slice DEFERS per
//   D2 (so algebra.dag is NOT imported now — it returns when D2
//   ratifies and the inhabitance rows land here), and std/node.dag
//   was named because rust.dag imports `Symbol` from it for its
//   lifetime axis (which Python doesn't have, so std/node.dag isn't
//   imported here either). The revised Consumes line above reflects
//   what the file actually imports — nothing. This Consumes
//   correction rides the systemic operator scaffold-Consumes
//   reconciliation, T-4 mgr synthesis msg_1fddb75c — the surface+
//   trigger discipline lets the operator ratify all per-file Consumes
//   strikes uniformly in one pass; this is not worker-settled. Same
//   discipline as ptx.dag #3170 (zero imports) and rust.dag #3174
//   (one import of Symbol for the lifetime axis Python lacks).)
//
// Status: T-4 python-slice modeled 2026-05-16 (gentle-deer-446);
//   structural finding: NO STOP from the type-system surface. The I/O
//   contract in this header is immutable for downstream workers.
