module v4.extdeps.languages.python


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// THE SLICE-VS-BUNDLED FRAMING (T-4, manager directive msg_4f7e1280
// fan-out from PR #3174). T-4 is the bundled work across the 5 Shape
// A source languages: "the SHAPE is the work" — establish the
// canonical declarative LanguageModel shape on one language (rust),
// then fan out to the other 4 (python is this fan-out instance).
// THIS slice is the python HALF of fix-the-shape-once: the type-
// system VOCABULARY and Python-side carrier shapes, with the D1/D2-
// dependent pieces (parse/emit operations + scalar algebra
// inhabitance instance-values) explicitly DEFERRED to their named
// owners. Deferral ≠ improvisation: every deferred item is named in
// "Owned ELSEWHERE" with its owner and dissolution trigger. The
// canonical-rust shape (PR #3174 loyal-koi-702) is mirrored here
// section-by-section; differences are STRUCTURAL Python-spec facts
// (no width axis; numeric subtype tower; three singletons; no
// reference-kind / no lifetime parameter), not improvisations.
//
// THE PYTHON → SUBSTRATE STRUCTURAL FIT. Each Python surface concept
// maps to a member of the closed substrate axes (node.dag's 6
// connectives + 5 L1 behaviors). The mapping, member-by-member —
// recording it here because the fit IS the deliverable (per the
// slice's structural finding above):
//
//   - FUNCTION (`def name(args) -> T: body`) ↦ Transform.
//     A Python `def` is a typed function — `(args) -> T` — whose
//     body is a sub-DAG of behaviors. That is exactly Transform (the
//     typed-total-λ fragment INVARIANTS:72; node.dag:209 "Transform
//     = application"). The function call site is also Transform:
//     the callee is a function value, the args are operands, the
//     result binds at the call site. No Python-specific behavior
//     surfaces; `def` IS Transform. (`async def`, generator functions
//     and async generators are DEFERRED — see "Owned ELSEWHERE"
//     coordination.dag T-4.8 ownership.)
//
//   - ASSIGNMENT (`x = expr`, `x: T = expr`) ↦ Bind. A Python
//     assignment introduces a name into a scope with a value; that is
//     exactly Bind (node.dag:209 "Bind = let"). Python's UNIVERSAL
//     name-binding semantics (a name in a scope is always rebindable;
//     `final` from `typing.Final` is a type-hint convention, not a
//     binding-level structural fact like Rust's `let mut`) means the
//     BINDING-LEVEL mutability axis Rust carries (`let` vs `let mut`)
//     COLLAPSES in Python — every binding is mutable at the
//     binding-level. The OBJECT-LEVEL mutability (which scalar kinds
//     admit in-place mutation: bytearray vs str/bytes) is a structural
//     fact on the OBJECT's type, declared via PythonStringKind below.
//
//   - MATCH (`match expr: case pat: ...`, PEP 634 / Python 3.10+) ↦
//     Branch. Python's match against a value is structural pattern
//     matching over class hierarchies, sequences, mappings, literals
//     — that is Branch over the value's structural coordinates
//     (node.dag:209 "Branch = sums"). Per Python Reference §8.6
//     `match` statements, match is NOT exhaustiveness-required —
//     when no case pattern matches, the spec says "the entire match
//     statement falls through" with no statement executed (this is
//     a deliberate contrast with rust's exhaustiveness-required
//     match, and a STRUCTURAL spec difference the model must
//     preserve: Python `match` does NOT enforce totality at the
//     spec level). The mapping to Branch is honest: Python `match`
//     is a Branch over the value's coordinates WITH an implicit
//     fall-through tail (the "no case matched" path — a no-op per
//     §8.6). A2 totality, when desired, is a CONSUMER property the
//     LanguageModel grammar layer (T-4 bundled) may opt into via a
//     match-exhaustiveness lens read or via a `case _:` wildcard
//     production — but the spec itself does NOT mandate it, and a
//     Python `match` without a wildcard is well-formed by the spec
//     even if no case fires. Predicated execution (`if cond: ...
//     elif: ... else: ...`) folds into the same Branch over a two-
//     variant `bool` Disj.
//     (Refactored 2026-05-16 per operator BLOCKING review on PR
//     #3180: the prior text claimed "match requires exhaustive
//     patterns" which is rust's spec, not Python's — INVARIANTS P1
//     modeling-faithfulness / L-2 extdeps fidelity violated. Python
//     Reference §8.6 explicitly admits fall-through.)
//
//   - LOOP (`for x in iter`, `while cond:`) ↦ Loop with BOUNDED
//     descent (node.dag:42-68, the A2 discipline). `for x in iter`
//     is bounded by the iterator's cardinality (a finite measure
//     when the iterator is bounded — a list / tuple / generator
//     with a known yield count); `while cond:` is bounded by a
//     ranking dimension that decreases on each iteration. Python
//     does NOT have a `loop { … break …; }` analog (no native
//     unbounded loop primitive; `while True:` is the idiom but it
//     is structurally `while` with a constant `True` condition —
//     under A2 it requires a break that makes the effective descent
//     bounded, otherwise STOP). The v4 Python model reads bounded
//     loops, fail-closes on structurally-unbounded ones.
//
//   - LITERAL / VALUE (`42`, `3.14`, `"hello"`, `True`, `None`,
//     `b"raw"`, `...`) ↦ Value (node.dag:209). A Python literal IS
//     a Value. The literal's typed interpretation (its scalar kind:
//     Int / Float / Bool / Complex / Str / Bytes / NoneT /
//     NotImplementedT / EllipsisT — the partition encoded by
//     PythonScalar below) is the inhabitance walk that RIDES
//     D2 — pre-D2 the Value's TYPED interpretation is unspecified;
//     the Value carrier itself is just a Node.
//
//   - CONTEXT MANAGER (`with cm as x: body`) ↦ Bind composed with
//     Branch (the cleanup path). The `with` statement is structural
//     sequencing — Bind-binding the context manager's __enter__
//     return value, then Branch'ing on the body's exit reason
//     (normal vs exception) to invoke __exit__. The exception-flow
//     half rides D1 (Outcome<T>) per "Owned ELSEWHERE." No Python-
//     specific behavior surfaces; `with` IS Bind + Branch.
//
//   - EXCEPTION FLOW (`raise`, `try / except / finally`) ↦ Bind +
//     Branch over Outcome<T>. Python's exception is a structural
//     error path on a typed return; once D1 (Outcome<T>) lands, a
//     function that `raise`s is one whose typed return is the
//     Rejected variant. `try: A except E: B` is Branch on the
//     Outcome: Produced -> A continuation, Rejected -> B with the
//     diagnostic bound. `finally:` is Bind over the cleanup. No new
//     behavior surfaces. The full mapping LANDS when D1 lands; this
//     slice declares the structural FIT only.
//
//   - REFERENCES (Python has no `&T` / `&mut T` distinction). Every
//     Python name is a reference; mutability is a property of the
//     OBJECT (the referent's type), not of the reference. The
//     rust analog (RustReferenceKind + RustReference, lifetime
//     Symbol) DOES NOT APPLY to Python — no PythonReferenceKind
//     carrier is declared, by spec. The OBJECT-level mutability
//     surfaces as the PythonStringKind partition (Str / Bytes
//     immutable vs Bytearray mutable) and at the container layer
//     (list / tuple / set / frozenset / dict — scope-negative for
//     this slice).
//
// CONCLUSION. The type-system half of Python's spec fits the
// existing substrate with no new connectives, no new behaviors, no
// parallel taxonomies. The classifiers declared below are the closed
// Python-side surface; their composition with grammar productions
// (Owned ELSEWHERE) and inhabitance instance-values (Owned ELSEWHERE
// — rides D2) lands when those dependencies dissolve, with the I/O
// contract of THIS file immutable.
//
// SCOPE NEGATIVE (operator-tier, do not work around).
//   - Container types (`list`, `tuple`, `set`, `frozenset`, `dict`,
//     `range`). Per the Reference §3.2 these are the SEQUENCE / SET /
//     MAPPING categories — NOT primitive scalars. Modeling them
//     requires a generic container-type carrier (Conj over an
//     element-type list + mutability tag + a Cardinality bound),
//     which composes over std/cardinality.dag's refinement substrate
//     (T-3) when that lands. Until then container types are out-of-
//     scope for this slice; emitting `[]` / `()` / `{}` as Value
//     literals is fine (they are Value Nodes) but the type carriers
//     are deferred to the container-types substrate. (Tuples in
//     particular: rust.dag #3174 declared its tuple-types
//     scope-negative under exactly this framing; Python tuples sit
//     in the same scope-negative bucket.)
//   - The full Python GRAMMAR production set (statement syntax,
//     expression syntax, comprehensions, pattern syntax, decorator
//     syntax, type-annotation syntax). Owned by the bundled T-4
//     LanguageModel shape — same framing as rust's "full Rust
//     GRAMMAR production set" and ptx's "warp-level intrinsics +
//     tensor cores" scope-negative. Not authored here. The
//     significant-whitespace-IS-block-structure C5-fidelity tag
//     (Reference §2.1.8 indentation; the canonical example in
//     TASKS.md T-4 authoring contract — "Python indentation IS
//     block structure") lands in the LanguageModel grammar
//     productions when that bundle lands.
//   - Python async / await semantics (`async def`, `.await`,
//     `Future`, coroutines, async iterators). These compose over the
//     type-system carriers declared here (an `async def` returns a
//     coroutine-typed value), but the coordination-effect typing is
//     owned by extdeps/coordination.dag (T-4.8, IN-B effect-typed
//     Bind carriers); not authored here.
//   - Python class hierarchies (`class X:` declarations, inheritance,
//     mixins, MRO, ABCs, metaclasses). Modeled at the LanguageModel
//     grammar layer (T-4 bundled work); the NOMINAL class-typed
//     value surface lands when grammar productions for class
//     declarations resolve. The numeric-tower partition declared
//     below IS the BUILT-IN-spec ABC hierarchy (PEP 3141), not the
//     general class-hierarchy carrier.
//   - Python type-annotation syntax (PEP 484 `typing` module, PEP
//     604 union types, PEP 612 ParamSpec, PEP 695 type parameter
//     syntax). These compose over the type-system carriers declared
//     here (annotations resolve to type-Node references), but the
//     SYNTAX is owned by the LanguageModel grammar layer (T-4
//     bundled); not authored here.
//   - Python protocols / structural typing (PEP 544
//     `typing.Protocol`). Runtime structural-compatibility checking
//     is a property of the LanguageModel grammar's type-annotation
//     productions, not of this scalar-vocabulary slice. The numeric
//     tower IS the BUILT-IN spec-ABC hierarchy; user-defined
//     protocols ride T-4 grammar.
//   - GIL / thread / multiprocessing semantics. Concurrency is owned
//     by extdeps/coordination.dag (T-4.8); not authored here.
//
// PRACTICE-4 LEDGER DISCIPLINE. Every closed-Disj carrier below
// carries the full 🟢/🟡/🔴 + all five patterns (Fact-placement /
// Variant-is-data / Algebraic-form / Dimensional / Parameterized-
// family-Practice-7) per modeling-discipline.md §4 (operator-ratified
// binding 2026-05-15, gate (b) — #3163 made the 4-pattern miss the
// proof case; do not omit Practice-7). Conj records carry no ledger
// (only coproducts dissolve); scaffold-residual Conj fields
// (PythonCost raw Int components) carry a 🟡 bridge note mirroring
// std/diagnostic.dag's `Extent.ByteRange`, ptx.dag's `Dim3` /
// `PtxCost`, and rust.dag's `RustCost` precedent.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PythonNumericTower — the closed Python numeric-subtype-tower
// classifier. Per Reference §3.2 numeric.Number ABC hierarchy (PEP
// 3141) the Python numeric tower has four levels; PEP 285 establishes
// `bool` as a subtype of `int` (so `True` and `False` are int values
// 1 and 0 respectively, and `bool` is the bottom of the tower).
//
//   - BoolLevel      — `bool` (the 2-valued numeric; PEP 285 — a
//                      strict subtype of Integral, NOT a parallel
//                      kind; True == 1 and False == 0 by spec)
//   - IntegralLevel  — `int` (arbitrary-precision integer; PEP 3141
//                      numbers.Integral)
//   - RealLevel      — `float` ("machine-level double precision" per
//                      Reference §3.2 the-standard-type-hierarchy
//                      — implementation-defined per the spec's
//                      "you are at the mercy of the underlying
//                      machine architecture" caveat; CPython
//                      realizes this as IEEE-754 binary64 but the
//                      spec does NOT mandate that fixed width.
//                      PEP 3141
//                      numbers.Real; the `Rational` ABC level lies
//                      between Integral and Real but has no built-in
//                      Python carrier — `fractions.Fraction` is
//                      stdlib not a primitive, and Decimal is
//                      explicitly NOT in the tower per PEP 3141; so
//                      the BUILT-IN primitive partition the spec
//                      pins is 4-level, not 5-level)
//   - ComplexLevel   — `complex` (a pair of `float` per Reference
//                      §3.2 — inheriting the same machine-level
//                      double precision + implementation caveat;
//                      CPython realizes it as a pair of IEEE-754
//                      binary64 but the spec does NOT mandate that
//                      fixed format. PEP 3141
//                      numbers.Complex; the top of the tower)
//
// The partial-order Bool ⊑ Integral ⊑ Real ⊑ Complex is
// STRUCTURALLY DERIVED from this closed enum (per the algebraic-form
// ledger entry below — pointing it elsewhere would parallel-represent
// the enumeration the order is defined over). The order is the
// numeric coercion path Python's arithmetic resolves through
// (Reference §6.16.1 arithmetic-conversions).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fifth level (e.g. promoting
// `fractions.Fraction` from stdlib library to primitive, or adding
// Decimal to the tower) is an edit to THIS declaration routed to
// operator ratification under the L-2 pin discipline (the
// `Wrapping<T>` / `Saturating<T>` rust analog — libraries are
// programs in the modeled language = Node per L-2, NOT additional
// tower levels).
//
// Spec: https://docs.python.org/3.14/reference/datamodel.html#numeric-types
//       (Reference §3.2 numeric-types) + PEP 3141
//       https://peps.python.org/pep-3141/ (Number ABC hierarchy)
//       + PEP 285 https://peps.python.org/pep-0285/ (`bool` as int
//       subtype).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every numeric-typed value consumer
//      (the Python `LanguageModel` grammar's numeric-literal
//      production, the per-target cost-realization for arithmetic
//      ops, the eventual inhabitance walk for Integral-vs-Real-vs-
//      Complex algebra dispatch) reads the tower level uniformly;
//      shared classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ level: Int }` proxy (0 / 1 / 2
//      / 3) replaces a closed 4-valued exhaustive sum with an
//      unbounded Int (level=42 representable) — violates P2 + M4
//      (closed sets are enums, not numeric proxies). Same shape as
//      algebra.dag's `Ordering` ledger and rust.dag's RustIntKind /
//      RustVisibility arguments.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: the four levels form the CONTAINMENT
//      partial-order Bool ⊑ Integral ⊑ Real ⊑ Complex (the
//      numeric-coercion order Reference §6.16.1 names); but the
//      partial-order is DERIVED FROM this closed enum, not a richer
//      source — pointing to a `BoundedLattice<PythonNumericTower>`
//      would parallel-represent the enumeration the order is defined
//      over (algebra.dag U1 engine-debt smell — the same argument
//      ptx.dag's BarrierScope and rust.dag's RustIntWidth /
//      RustVisibility ledgers make). The per-level algebra grounding
//      (BoolLevel → BooleanAlgebra; IntegralLevel → OrderedRing or
//      Semiring per the integer.dag carrier; RealLevel →
//      ApproximateField per the float.dag carrier; ComplexLevel →
//      ApproximateField over `Complex` extension) IS the inhabitance
//      walk owned by std/integer.dag / std/float.dag / std/logic.dag
//      per P2 (rides D2 + T-3); the tower enum itself stays a closed
//      classifier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Python numeric value has
//      EXACTLY ONE tower level; PEP 285's subtype relationship
//      (`bool` is-a `int`) is a SUBTYPE fact at the spec layer, not
//      a co-coordinate (a value typed `bool` IS a `bool` whose
//      subtype-tower view yields `Integral`; the bottom-most-level
//      principal type is unique). Mutually-exclusive alternatives,
//      not simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the four levels are the PEP
//      3141 + PEP 285 built-in-primitive tower partition; they are
//      NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-
//      declared source set (Practice 7). The tower levels correspond
//      to STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT algebraic groundings (BooleanAlgebra
//      / OrderedRing / ApproximateField over Real / ApproximateField
//      over Complex), not projection-uniform over any richer source.
//      The four levels are the spec's irreducible numeric-tower
//      partition for the built-in primitive set.
// Terminal: the closed four-level Python built-in numeric-tower
// partition for the pinned Python Reference; a 5th is the C1
// stop-signal (a visible ratified edit to this declaration,
// STRUCTURE.md commitment #1).
type PythonNumericTower
  = BoolLevel
  | IntegralLevel
  | RealLevel
  | ComplexLevel


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PythonStringKind — the closed Python textual / byte-sequence kind
// classifier. Per Reference §3.2 the-standard-type-hierarchy
// sequences section, Python's primitive string-like types partition
// into exactly three kinds; the mutability tag is FIXED per kind by
// spec (Str and Bytes are immutable sequence types; Bytearray is the
// only mutable byte-sequence primitive), so this enum's variants
// encode mutability admissibility STRUCTURALLY — a "Bytes that is
// mutable" combination is unrepresentable.
//
//   - Str        — `str`       Unicode string (immutable;
//                              FreeMonoid<UnicodeScalarValue> per the
//                              Reference §3.2 textual-types section
//                              and PEP 3100; `str` was unified to
//                              Unicode in Python 3)
//   - Bytes      — `bytes`     immutable byte sequence
//                              (FreeMonoid<Byte>)
//   - Bytearray  — `bytearray` mutable byte sequence (the ONLY
//                              mutable scalar primitive in Python's
//                              spec; the structural mutability fact
//                              that distinguishes this variant from
//                              Bytes)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fourth string-kind primitive (e.g.
// memoryview promoted to primitive — currently a built-in but
// modeled as a view / buffer-protocol carrier, not a primitive byte
// sequence) is an edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin
// discipline.
//
// Spec: https://docs.python.org/3.14/reference/datamodel.html#types
//       (Reference §3.2 textual / sequence types — Str / Bytes /
//       Bytearray subsections) + https://peps.python.org/pep-3100/
//       (Python 3 str/bytes unification).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every string-typed-value consumer
//      (literal parsing — the `"…"` / `b"…"` / `bytearray(b"…")`
//      productions; emission of textual / byte-sequence syntax;
//      cost-realization for sequence operations) reads the string
//      kind uniformly; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ kind: Symbol, mutable: Bool }`
//      proxy reintroduces TWO illegal states the closed Disj
//      forbids: (a) a readable-string-as-tag heuristic surface K-1
//      forecloses (a `match kind { "str" => … }` smell), and (b)
//      the "Bytes with mutable=true" combination that the Reference
//      partitions as a separate type (Bytearray). The 3-variant
//      Disj with FIXED-mutability-per-variant IS the dissolved form
//      (P2 + Practice 2/6 + M4).
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each kind grounds in a free-monoid
//      construction (Str → FreeMonoid<UnicodeScalarValue> per the
//      Reference's Unicode textual semantics; Bytes / Bytearray →
//      FreeMonoid<Byte>, with Bytearray additionally inhabiting
//      MutableSequence — a Cardinality + interior-mutation
//      structural fact). That grounding IS the inhabitance walk
//      owned by std/text.dag / std/cardinality.dag (T-3 wave A3)
//      per P2; the kind enum stays a closed classifier; the "kind
//      → algebra" map is the future inhabitance walk, not a richer
//      std/ source to collapse this enum INTO.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Python string-typed value has
//      EXACTLY ONE kind; `b"raw"` is Bytes (not Str-with-mutable-
//      view); `bytearray(…)` is Bytearray (not Bytes-with-mutability-
//      flag). The kind × mutability admissibility is structurally
//      determined by the kind alone (mutability is fixed per kind);
//      there are no simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three kinds are the
//      Python Reference's primitive-string-like partition; they are
//      NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-
//      declared source set (Practice 7). The variants correspond to
//      STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT character-codomains (UnicodeScalarValue
//      for Str; Byte for Bytes / Bytearray) AND structurally
//      distinct mutability disciplines (immutable for Str / Bytes;
//      mutable for Bytearray) — they are not projection-uniform
//      over any richer source.
// Terminal: the closed three-way Python primitive-string-kind
// classifier with structural mutability discrimination; the
// Reference §3.2 partition is irreducible at this scalar layer.
type PythonStringKind
  = Str
  | Bytes
  | Bytearray


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PythonSingletonKind — the closed Python singleton-type classifier.
// Per Reference §3.2 the-standard-type-hierarchy, Python defines
// exactly three singleton types — each has its own type (NoneType,
// NotImplementedType, EllipsisType) with EXACTLY ONE inhabitant
// each. The empty-inhabitance fact (each singleton-type's inhabitant
// count is structurally 1) RIDES std/cardinality.dag's
// one-cardinality carrier (T-3 wave A3) when that lands.
//
//   - NoneT             — `None` (the absence-of-value singleton;
//                         `NoneType`); used as the default return
//                         value of a `def` without explicit return
//                         and as the absence-of-value placeholder
//                         in Python's standard-library APIs
//   - NotImplementedT   — `NotImplemented` (the
//                         operator-method-fallback singleton; an
//                         arithmetic / comparison dunder method
//                         returns `NotImplemented` to signal the
//                         operation is not implemented for the
//                         given operand types, prompting Python's
//                         reverse-method-dispatch / fallback chain
//                         per Reference §3.3.7
//                         numeric-coercion-rules)
//   - EllipsisT         — `...` / `Ellipsis` (the
//                         slice-placeholder / type-annotation
//                         singleton; used in slicing syntax
//                         `arr[...]`, in type annotations like
//                         `Callable[..., T]`, and in stub files for
//                         placeholder bodies per PEP 484)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fourth singleton primitive (e.g. a new
// `Missing` sentinel promoted to spec) is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Spec: https://docs.python.org/3.14/reference/datamodel.html#the-standard-type-hierarchy
//       (Reference §3.2 — None / NotImplemented / Ellipsis
//       subsections) + Reference §6.2.1 atom-identifiers for the
//       singleton-identifier resolution rules.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every singleton-typed-value consumer
//      (literal parsing — the `None` / `NotImplemented` / `...`
//      productions; emission of singleton syntax; reverse-method-
//      dispatch on `NotImplemented` return; slicing-syntax
//      `Ellipsis` handling) reads the singleton kind uniformly;
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ name: Symbol }` proxy reopens
//      the readable-string heuristic surface K-1 forecloses (a
//      `match name { "None" => … }` smell, exactly the v3
//      regression the K-1 opacity discipline structurally
//      prevents). The enum IS the faithful form. AND collapsing
//      the three singletons to a single `Singleton { which: Symbol
//      }` carrier ratifies arbitrary user-introduced singletons —
//      the Reference partitions exactly THREE, and additional
//      sentinels are library / user code (e.g. `unittest.mock
//      .DEFAULT`), NOT spec primitives.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the three singletons
//      do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure (each is
//      a one-inhabitant Cardinality fact, owned by
//      std/cardinality.dag's one-cardinality carrier per P2). The
//      structural fact "exactly one inhabitant" is a CARDINALITY
//      witness on each singleton; the kind enum itself is the
//      partition the witnesses ride, not a richer collapsing
//      source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Python singleton value has
//      EXACTLY ONE kind; `None` is `NoneT`-typed, never
//      simultaneously `NotImplementedT`-typed. Mutually-exclusive
//      alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three singletons are the
//      Python Reference's three spec-defined singleton-type
//      partition; they are NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over
//      a separately-declared source set (Practice 7). One could
//      attempt `Singleton<role: SingletonRole>` parameterized over a
//      3-element role set, but the roles are
//      STRUCTURALLY-DISTINCT spec-defined sentinels with
//      heterogeneous purposes (absence-of-value /
//      operator-fallback / slice-placeholder) — not projection-
//      uniform over any richer source. The three are the spec's
//      irreducible singleton partition.
// Terminal: the closed three-way Python primitive-singleton-kind
// classifier; a 4th is the C1 stop-signal.
type PythonSingletonKind
  = NoneT
  | NotImplementedT
  | EllipsisT


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PythonScalar — the closed Python top-level primitive-scalar
// typed-value Disj. Per Reference §3.2 the-standard-type-hierarchy
// the BUILT-IN primitive scalars partition into exactly three
// categories — Numeric (the four numeric-tower levels), String (the
// three textual / byte-sequence kinds), and Singleton (the three
// spec-defined one-inhabitant types).
//
// SINGLE-CARRIER CONSOLIDATION (the Python-vs-rust structural
// difference, naming-drift correction per operator BLOCKING review
// codex on PR #3180). The rust.dag #3174 precedent declares TWO
// scalar carriers — RustScalarKind (the flat 6-way meta-enum
// classifier) and RustScalar (the Disj-with-payloads typed-value
// form with kind×width admissibility). Those two are at different
// layers in rust because the typed-value form needs WIDTH payloads
// the meta-classifier doesn't. Python has NO orthogonal width axis
// (see header Python-particulars (a) — int is arbitrary-precision,
// float is machine-level double precision, no per-kind width
// discriminator). So the meta-classifier and the typed-value form
// COLLAPSE INTO ONE Disj in Python: PythonScalar's variants
// (Numeric / String / Singleton) ARE the 3-category meta-tags AND
// the typed-value variants in one — there is no separate
// `PythonScalarKind` because there is no orthogonal axis for it
// to factor across. The earlier doc references to a separate
// `PythonScalarKind` were doc-vs-code drift; PythonScalar is the
// single canonical carrier (P1 modeling-faithfulness / live-state
// documentation per the codex finding that drove this correction).
//
// The variant payloads carry the sub-classifier kind, making the
// Cartesian product of "category × in-category-kind" STRUCTURALLY
// ENFORCED — an illegal cross-category combination like "a
// Singleton with a numeric tower level" is unrepresentable (the
// INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable shape, the same
// design choice rust.dag's RustScalar took with kind×width payloads
// and ptx.dag's RegisterScalar took with kind × width ×
// state_space).
//
//   - Numeric    — { tower: PythonNumericTower }
//                  e.g. Numeric { tower: IntegralLevel } is Python's
//                  `int`; Numeric { tower: BoolLevel } is `bool` —
//                  whose subtype relationship to IntegralLevel is
//                  the tower's structural fact, not a parallel kind.
//   - String     — { kind: PythonStringKind }
//                  e.g. String { kind: Str } is Python's `str`;
//                  String { kind: Bytearray } is `bytearray` — whose
//                  mutability is the kind's structural fact.
//   - Singleton  — { kind: PythonSingletonKind }
//                  e.g. Singleton { kind: NoneT } is the type of
//                  `None`; Singleton { kind: EllipsisT } is the type
//                  of `...`.
//
// The structural argument for the Disj-with-payloads (vs rust's
// `RustScalarKind` flat 6-way enum and ptx's `RegisterScalarKind`
// flat 5-way enum): rust and ptx have FLAT primitive partitions
// where every variant's structural shape is the same kind tag (rust's
// Int / Float / Bool / Char / Str / Never; ptx's Bits / Unsigned /
// Signed / Float / Predicate). Python's primitive partition is
// HIERARCHICAL — a three-category top with structurally distinct
// sub-classifiers per category (numeric tower / string kind /
// singleton kind). A flat 10-variant enum (Int / Float / Bool /
// Complex / Str / Bytes / Bytearray / NoneT / NotImplementedT /
// EllipsisT) would lose the category structure the Reference makes
// explicit; a Conj record `{ category, tower?, string_kind?,
// singleton_kind? }` would ratify illegal cross-category
// combinations. The 3-category Disj-with-payloads is the dissolved
// form: each variant carries EXACTLY the sub-classifier its category
// admits, and no illegal cross-category combination is constructible.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fourth top-level category is an edit
// to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline (the Reference's
// SCALAR partition has been stable at Numeric / String / Singleton +
// the SEQUENCE / SET / MAPPING / CALLABLE / MODULE / CLASS / INSTANCE
// container categories — the latter scope-negative for this slice;
// container types are not scalars).
//
// Spec: https://docs.python.org/3.14/reference/datamodel.html#the-standard-type-hierarchy
//       (Reference §3.2 the standard type hierarchy — the top-level
//       partition this enum encodes).
//
// SEAM (where D2 inhabitance plugs in). When the D2 form decision
// lands (operator-pending, msg_640d429c), the per-variant
// inhabitance instance-values land in this file with these typed
// variants as their carriers — Numeric { tower: IntegralLevel } is
// the carrier for Python's `int` inhabitance into Integral (or its
// v4-equivalent per the D2-ratified form); String { kind: Str } is
// the carrier for `str` → FreeMonoid<UnicodeScalarValue>; Singleton
// { kind: NoneT } is the carrier for `None` → one-cardinality. The
// seam itself is the typed variant; the inhabitance value is the
// deferred row.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Python-typed value consumer
//      (literal parsing, expression typing, emission to source,
//      cost-realization, eventual inhabitance dispatch) reads the
//      scalar category + sub-kind through this carrier; shared,
//      not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing the variants to a
//      uniform record `{ category: PythonCategory, tower:
//      Option<…>, string_kind: Option<…>, singleton_kind:
//      Option<…> }` reintroduces illegal cross-category
//      combinations (a Singleton carrying a numeric tower level;
//      a Numeric with no tower set) the 3-variant Disj-with-
//      payloads exists to forbid. The 3-variant Disj IS the
//      dissolved form (P2 + Practice 2/6 + M4 — same argument as
//      rust.dag RustScalar).
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra or cardinality structure (Numeric → per-tower-level
//      algebra per the PythonNumericTower ledger; String → free-
//      monoid construction per the PythonStringKind ledger;
//      Singleton → one-cardinality per std/cardinality.dag). That
//      grounding IS the inhabitance walk owned ELSEWHERE per P2;
//      the Disj structure here is the closed surface, not a
//      collapsing target.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Python scalar value IS
//      EXACTLY ONE variant of this Disj; mutually-exclusive
//      alternatives. The shapes differ across variants (each
//      carries its category-specific sub-classifier; the categories
//      are disjoint) — they cannot be coordinates of a single
//      record without the illegal-state smell.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three category variants
//      are NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-
//      declared source set (Practice 7). The PTX RegisterScalar
//      pattern (Conj-record over (kind, width, state_space)) was
//      projection-uniform; the rust RustScalar pattern (Disj-with-
//      width-payload-per-kind) was projection-non-uniform over the
//      kind enum; Python's PythonScalar pattern is projection-
//      non-uniform over a HIERARCHICAL category × sub-kind
//      structure — each category's sub-classifier is structurally
//      distinct (PythonNumericTower / PythonStringKind /
//      PythonSingletonKind ground in three different
//      std/ structures: algebra-tower / free-monoid / cardinality).
//      The Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads is the irreducible
//      faithful form for the Reference's hierarchical partition.
// Terminal: the closed three-category Python primitive-scalar
// typed-value sum; illegal cross-category combinations are
// unrepresentable by construction; container types (list / tuple /
// set / frozenset / dict / range) are out-of-scope for this slice
// (SCOPE NEGATIVE container types in the header).
type PythonScalar
  = Numeric { tower: PythonNumericTower }
  | String { kind: PythonStringKind }
  | Singleton { kind: PythonSingletonKind }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PythonCost — the per-target cost-realization carrier shape for the
// Python `LanguageModel`. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
// This declares the SHAPE the U1 spine reads when computing Python-
// side realization cost — both components per the scaffold header's
// "cost realization for Python" claim (preserved as a goal; the
// carrier shape lands now, the cost INSTANCE-VALUES land downstream
// per "Owned ELSEWHERE"):
//
//   - operation_cost  per-spec-operation throughput cost (the U1
//                     per-target-op cost projection — the `map` side
//                     of `Homomorphism<…, …, PythonScalar>` carries
//                     this; cost is read FROM the map's structure,
//                     never stored as a parallel field per
//                     algebra.dag's U1 no-engine discipline. The
//                     operation set this measures is the Python
//                     LANGUAGE REFERENCE's §6 expressions / §8
//                     compound-statements partition — the spec-
//                     level operations, NOT the CPython BYTECODE.
//                     CPython's bytecode (the `dis` module's opcode
//                     set) is an IMPLEMENTATION DETAIL the
//                     standard library explicitly disclaims (the
//                     `dis` docs: "CPython bytecode... is an
//                     implementation detail of the CPython
//                     interpreter. No guarantees are made that
//                     bytecode will not be added, removed, or
//                     changed between versions of Python"). Other
//                     Python implementations (PyPy / Jython /
//                     GraalPython / MicroPython) have entirely
//                     different VMs and operation sets; modeling
//                     CPython bytecode as the cost-authority would
//                     violate the L-2 fidelity-to-the-LANGUAGE-
//                     REFERENCE pin this file declares (operator
//                     BLOCKING on PR #3180 made this precise).
//                     The natural Python-side per-instruction
//                     analog of rust.dag's `instruction_cost` and
//                     ptx.dag's `instruction_cost` is the spec-
//                     operation cost, NOT the bytecode cost.
//   - allocation_cost per-allocation cost (the Python-specific
//                     contribution: Python heap-allocates objects
//                     liberally — almost every spec-operation creates
//                     a new object reference; the per-allocation cost
//                     is structurally distinct from per-spec-operation
//                     throughput and the per-target shape the cost
//                     lens T-12 reads when synthesizing a Python
//                     realization's total cost. The rust analog is
//                     `RustCost.allocation_cost` — Python and Rust
//                     both carry an allocation-cost component but
//                     with very different magnitudes per operation;
//                     the SHAPE is the same, the instance-values
//                     differ per target.)
//
// 🟡 SCAFFOLD (same bridge as std/diagnostic.dag's `Extent.ByteRange`,
// ptx.dag's `Dim3` / `PtxCost`, and rust.dag's `RustCost`). With only
// kernel-ambient `Int` available at the T-4 phase, `{ operation_cost:
// Int, allocation_cost: Int }` STRUCTURALLY ADMITS illegal states
// (negative costs; nonsense magnitudes). modeling-discipline.md §2
// (illegal-states-unrepresentable) forbids admitting them; no
// bounded-Cardinality refinement substrate exists yet to exclude
// them. It is a TRACKED scaffold, declared with the three bridge
// properties (the precedent diagnostic.dag established 2026-05-15):
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the carrier is provisional, not
//       the terminal refined-positive shape.
//   (b) bounds on use — a producer MUST construct only non-negative
//       costs; until the substrate enforces it the invariant is
//       producer-side; a PythonCost with negative components is a
//       producer bug, not a legal value.
//   (c) dissolution trigger — when std/cardinality.dag's refinement
//       substrate lands (T-3, P4 decidability), `operation_cost` and
//       `allocation_cost` become refined bounded-non-negative-Int
//       axes, and this scaffold dissolves.
//
// SEAM (where T-12 cost-lens reads). When lens/cost.dag (T-12, U2)
// lands, its `SymbolicCost` lattice carrier composes over the per-
// target projections; the Python projection is read from this Conj
// record's components per the U2 "complexity consumes cost, never
// re-derives" discipline. Pre-T-12, PythonCost is the declared
// SHAPE the projection plugs into; concrete cost instance-values per
// spec-operation (e.g. an integer-binary-add per Reference §6.6
// arithmetic-conversions costs `PythonCost { operation_cost: 1,
// allocation_cost: 0 }` for in-place numeric ops vs `PythonCost
// { operation_cost: 1, allocation_cost: 1 }` for ops that produce a
// new object) ride T-12 + D2 per "Owned ELSEWHERE." Concrete cost
// rows MUST cite a Reference §6/§8 spec operation, NOT a CPython
// bytecode opcode — the cost authority is the language reference,
// not any particular VM.
type PythonCost {
  operation_cost: Int
  allocation_cost: Int
}
